  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Sunday...\n\nLow clouds/stratus were gradually diminishing over central areas \nthis afternoon as ascent increases from the west with an\napproaching trough/cold front. Expect stratus to linger over the\neast into the evening, with low clouds and localized fog\ncontinuing overnight into Tuesday morning. Greatest chance for fog\nis over far southeast MT (40% chance). \n\nAn upper trough and associated Pacific cold front will begin \nmoving into the west this evening, bringing rain/mountain snow \nshowers, and even the potential for a few thunderstorms. The\nshowers will spread east into the overnight hours, reaching\nBillings after midnight, gradually spreading north and east into \nthe day Tuesday, as the trough crosses Montana. Liquid\nprecipitation amounts will range from a few hundredths to a tenth\nover the lower elevations west of Fallon/Carter counties, to 0.25\non western aspects of the mountains. A few inches of snow are \npossible on the western aspects of the Crazy and \nBeartooth/Absaroka mountains, generally above 7,000 ft. Snow may \nmix in with the rain into the foothills due to the convective \nnature of the showers, but surface temperatures should remain warm\nenough to prevent accumulations below 5,000 ft. I-90 over Bozeman\nPass could see difficult travel conditions briefly later this \nevening.\n\nBehind the front, pressure rises and increasing winds at the mid-\nlevels will bring breezy westerly winds to the area Tuesday. Most\nlocations will see wind gusts of 20-40 mph, with wind gusts of\n40-50 mph along the western foothills. The probability of\nexceeding 60 mph from Livingston to Big Timber and Harlowton is\nroughly 30 percent or less. With the downslope flow, temperatures\nwill top out in the upper 40s to upper 50s. Winds decrease into \nTuesday evening.\n\nFor the second half of the work week into the weekend, the \npattern looks to transition to southwest flow aloft over the \nregion. This will allow occasional rain/snow showers over the \nwestern mountains and foothills, with the lower elevations\nremaining mostly dry and breezy at times. High temperatures will\nremain near to slightly above normal (highs mainly in the 50s)\nthrough Thursday, cooling into the 40s to 50s beyond. While still\naways out, there is potential for more unsettled conditions \nSunday into early next week as ensembles and clusters prog a \nlarge trough to push into the Northern Rockies. STP",
